{
  "quizSrc": [
   {
      "term": "According to Anthony Ashley Cooper, 3rd Earl of Shaftesbury, what is the basis of our perception of beauty? A. Imagination B. Personal experience. C. The innate concept. D. Imagination and Personal experience.",
      "definition": "C. The innate concept."
    },
    {
      "term": "How does Aristotle view the role of the artist in imitating reality? A. The artist should only create perfect replicas of the real world. B. The artist should focus on portraying the negative aspects of reality. C. The artist can improve upon reality by incorporating universals. D. The artist should primarily document historical events.",
      "definition": "C. The artist can improve upon reality by incorporating universals."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is the key difference between Aristotle's view on beauty and the views of his predecessors? A. He believes beauty is based on objective qualities, not subjective experience. B. He emphasizes the importance of moderation in achieving beauty. C. He defines beauty solely in terms of size and order. D. He rejects the Pythagorean connection of beauty to mathematics.",
      "definition": "B. He emphasizes the importance of moderation in achieving beauty."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is the purpose of an ‘Introduction: Aesthetics and the Philosophy of Art, An Extremely Brief History’? A. Provide a comprehensive and complete history of Western aesthetics. B. Highlighting some central themes in the Western aesthetic tradition. C. Explain the difference between 'aesthetics' and 'philosophy of art'. D. Analyzing contemporary works of art involves examining various aspects",
      "definition": "B. Highlighting some central themes in the Western aesthetic tradition."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is the role of music according to Pythagoras? A. As a form of entertainment. B. As a tool to explore order and purify the soul. C. As a means of self-expression. D. As a means to earn money.",
      "definition": "B. As a tool to explore order and purify the soul."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is the main difference between Pythagoras's view and Gorgias's view of the impact of art? A. Pythagoras believed that art has a positive effect, whereas Gorgias believed that art has a negative effect. B. Pythagoras believed that art has a lasting effect, while Gorgias believed that art has a transient effect. C. Pythagoras believed that art affects the soul, while Gorgias believed that art affects emotions. D. Pythagoras believed that art has a purifying effect, while Gorgias believed that art has a stimulating effect.",
      "definition": "B. Pythagoras believed that art has a lasting effect, while Gorgias believed that art has a transient effect."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is the relationship between 'Aesthetics' and 'The Philosophy of Art'? A. The two terms are completely different and unrelated. B. Two terms are often used interchangeably to refer to the same field of study. C. \"Aesthetics\" is a subfield of \"philosophy of art.\" D. \"The philosophy of art\" is a subfield of \"aesthetics.\"",
      "definition": "B. Two terms are often used interchangeably to refer to the same field of study."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is Aristotle's main disagreement with Plato regarding poetry? A. Poetry should focus on reason rather than emotion. B. Poetry is a poor representation of reality. C. Poetry has a negative impact on the human condition. D. Poetry is not a uniquely human characteristic.",
      "definition": "C. Poetry has a negative impact on the human condition."
    },
    {
      "term": "What did Marsilio Ficino do that contributed to the revival of Platonic thought? A. Criticized Plato's theories in favor of other classical thinkers. B. Translated Plato's complete works into a spoken language. C. Developed entirely original theories unrelated to Plato. D. Argued against the Neoplatonist concept of beauty.",
      "definition": "B. Translated Plato's complete works into a spoken language."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Marcilio Ficino, how do we perceive beauty? A. Through the perfect forms of beauty found in the things themselves. B. Through a mental alignment with our innate ideas of beauty. C. Through the skill of the artist in capturing perfect forms. D. Through the divine inspiration that strikes the poet during creation.",
      "definition": "B. Through a mental alignment with our innate ideas of beauty."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Monroe C. Beardsley, can a toaster be considered a work of art? A. Yes, if it's designed by a highly intelligent Bauhaus designer. B. Yes, if it is intended to satisfy aesthetic preferences. C. No, because it does not create an aesthetic effect for the viewer. D. No, because it is not an object created by an artist.",
      "definition": "B. Yes, if it is intended to satisfy aesthetic preferences."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to R. G. Collingwood, what makes true art? A. Arouse emotions in the audience. B. Entertain the audience C. Showcase the artist's skills D. To reflect reality honestly",
      "definition": "C. Showcase the artist's skills"
    },
    {
      "term": "Fill the blank Berys Gaut argues against a global theory of interpretation in his essay \"Interpreting the Arts: The Patchwork Theory\" because:… A. Intentionalist theories are always correct. B. There are no acceptable consequences of interpreting an author's intent. C. Authors can fail to achieve their intended meaning in a work. D. The text itself can contribute to meaning, regardless of authorial intent.",
      "definition": "D. The text itself can contribute to meaning, regardless of authorial intent."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which approach to determining the meaning of a literary work does Jerrold Levinson advocate for? A. Word-sequence meaning B. Utterer's meaning C. Author's intention D. Utterance meaning",
      "definition": "D. Utterance meaning"
    },
    {
      "term": "Which theory does Berys Gaut challenge in his essay 'Interpreting the Arts: The Patchwork Theory'? A. Global theory of interpretation B. Intentionalist theory C. Anti-intentionalist theory D. Hypothetical intentionalist theory",
      "definition": "A. Global theory of interpretation"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Paul Ziff, can a true definition of art be found? A. Yes B. No. C. Not for sure. D. Not mentioned",
      "definition": "B. No."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which film does Carroll use as an example to discuss the idea of subversive filmmaking? A. \"Plan 9 From Outer Space\" B. \"Night of the Living Dead\" C. \"The Day the Earth Stood Still\" D. \"The War of the Worlds\"",
      "definition": "A. \"Plan 9 From Outer Space\""
    },
    {
      "term": "How does Levinson define a new work of art? A. Some exhibited features of the artwork. B. Its relationship with a formal or informal artistic organization. C. Its relationship with pre-existing art. D. Our experience of the artwork.",
      "definition": "C. Its relationship with pre-existing art."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to George Dickie, what factor constitutes a work of art? A. Aesthetics B. Ideas C. The recognition by the art world. D. The material value of the artwork.",
      "definition": "C. The recognition by the art world."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which approach does Berys Gaut advocate for in interpretation theories? A. Intentionalism B. Anti-intentionalism C. Hypothetical intentionalism D. Reader-response criticism",
      "definition": "C. Hypothetical intentionalism"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Berys Gaut, intentionalist critics like Hirsch and anti-intentionalist critics like Wimsatt and Beardsley are trying to do? A. Explaining the meaning of a work of art. B. Analysis of various aspects of interpretation C. To find a universal theory of interpretation D. Evaluating the value of a work of art",
      "definition": "B. Analysis of various aspects of interpretation"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Levinson, who is the \"appropriate\" reader to interpret a literary work? A. The reader with knowledge of the literary tradition to which the work belongs. B. The reader familiar with other works by the author. C. The reader knowledgeable about the public persona of the author. D. All of the above.",
      "definition": "D. All of the above."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to the anti-intentionalist perspective of Wimsatt and Beardsley, which interpretation of the film \"Plan 9 from Outer Space\" is correct? A. The interpretation intended by the author. B. The interpretation that best fits the artwork. C. The interpretation agreed upon by film critics. D. The interpretation that makes the film successful.",
      "definition": "B. The interpretation that best fits the artwork."
    },
    {
      "term": "The claim \"Last Leaf\" is a song about death according to Krausz's theory, is it correct? A. True, because it adds to the meaning of the work. B. False, because it changes the meaning of the work. C. Cannot be determined, because the meaning of the work is not predetermined. D. Depends on the context.",
      "definition": "C. Cannot be determined, because the meaning of the work is not predetermined."
    },
    {
      "term": "In the anti-intentionalist perspective, what is the \"correct\" interpretation of a work of art? A. The interpretation intended by the author. B. There is no interpretation that is \"correct.\" C. The interpretation by critics. D. The interpretation that best fits the artwork.",
      "definition": "B. There is no interpretation that is \"correct.\""
    },
    {
      "term": "How is Tom Waits's music often described? A. Accessible and market-friendly B. Smooth and melodious C. Hard to listen to and incomprehensible D. Either accessible or very difficult to access",
      "definition": "D. Either accessible or very difficult to access"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to critic Daniel Durchholz, how was Tom Waits's singing voice developed? A. Through extensive vocal training and classical techniques B. By performing in smoky bars and clubs for many years C. By soaking his voice in a vat of bourbon, leaving it in a smokehouse, and running it over with a car D. Through a combination of natural talent and professional coaching",
      "definition": "D. Through a combination of natural talent and professional coaching"
    },
    {
      "term": "What is one characteristic often used to describe Tom Waits's albums? A. Mainstream appeal and radio-friendliness B. Smooth and polished production quality C. Varied levels of accessibility, ranging from commercially friendly to highly eclectic D. Traditional and conventional songwriting structures",
      "definition": "C. Varied levels of accessibility, ranging from commercially friendly to highly eclectic"
    },
    {
      "term": "How is Tom Waits's music often perceived by both critics and fellow musicians? A. As strictly adhering to traditional musical styles and conventions B. As predominantly blues ballads or sea shanties, reflecting his diverse influences C. As adventure songs and Halloween music, combining tradition with innovation D. As exclusively belonging to a cult following, without broader appeal",
      "definition": "C. As adventure songs and Halloween music, combining tradition with innovation"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to which view is the meaning of a work of art primarily determined by? A. The author's intention B. The content of the work C. The reader D. All three factors above",
      "definition": "D. All three factors above"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Robert Stecker, what is the ultimate purpose of our interest in experiencing art? A. The purpose of art. B. The intrinsic value of art. C. The joy that art brings. D. The utilitarian value of art.",
      "definition": "C. The joy that art brings."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to contextualists, what is the main difference between an original work of art and a derivative work? A. The value of the artwork. B. The reputation of the author. C. The creative conditions of the artwork. D. The aesthetic properties of the artwork.",
      "definition": "C. The creative conditions of the artwork."
    },
    {
      "term": "Fill the blank: The most important thing, according to Pettit, to experience a work of art is... A. Understanding the author and the artwork. B. Experiencing the artwork under suitable conditions. C. Having knowledge about art. D. Having well-functioning senses.",
      "definition": "B. Experiencing the artwork under suitable conditions."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Kant, how can we evaluate the beauty of an object? A. By comparing it to a definite concept of beauty. B. By using simple rules to evaluate its qualities. C. By using intuition. D. By comparing it to other objects.",
      "definition": "C. By using intuition."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Francis Hutcheson, where is beauty found? A. Proportion. B. \"Uniformity amidst variety\". C. Harmony. D. Balance.",
      "definition": "B. \"Uniformity amidst variety\"."
    },
    {
      "term": "Pythagoras and his followers believed that beauty arose from…? A. Irregularity and complexity. B. harmony, order, and regularity. C. Personal emotions and intuition. D. Cultural and social factors.",
      "definition": "B. harmony, order, and regularity."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is Leon Battista Alberti's view on beauty? A. Beauty is subjective and varies greatly among individuals. B. Judgments of beauty are based on an innate sense of beauty and lead to universal agreement on what is beautiful. C. Beauty can only be appreciated by those with a particularly acute sense of taste. D. The sense of beauty is entirely separate from the moral sense.",
      "definition": "B. Judgments of beauty are based on an innate sense of beauty and lead to universal agreement on what is beautiful."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Sibley, what is the relationship between the non-aesthetic and aesthetic qualities of a work of art? A. Non-aesthetic qualities completely determine the aesthetic qualities of a work. B. Non-aesthetic qualities have no relation to the aesthetic qualities of a work. C. Non-aesthetic qualities can influence aesthetic qualities but do not guarantee them. D. Non-aesthetic qualities always exclude certain aesthetic qualities.",
      "definition": "C. Non-aesthetic qualities can influence aesthetic qualities but do not guarantee them."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Jerrold Levinson, what determines the meaning of a literary work? A. Word-sequence meaning. B. Utterer's meaning. C. Utterance meaning. D. Autonomy of the text.",
      "definition": "C. Utterance meaning."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which term from the passage typically denotes a value judgment when assessing artworks? A. Elegant B. Black C. Square D. Imposing",
      "definition": "A. Elegant"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to the anti-intentionalist perspective of Wimsatt and Beardsley, which interpretation of the film \"Plan 9 from Outer Space\" is correct? A. The interpretation by the author B. The interpretation consistent with the artwork. C. The interpretation agreed upon by film critics. D. The interpretation that makes the film successful.",
      "definition": "B. The interpretation consistent with the artwork."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to radical constructivism, when two people interpret the same work of art, will they create two different meanings? A. They will create the same artwork. B. They will create two different artworks. C. They will not create any artwork. D. They will destroy the artwork.",
      "definition": "B. They will create two different artworks."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Margolis, what would happen if all the tokens of an artwork were destroyed? A. The artwork will still exist. B. The artwork will be replaced by a new artwork. C. The original artwork will cease to exist. D. The artwork will be converted into a different form of art.",
      "definition": "C. The original artwork will cease to exist."
    },
    {
      "term": "Fill the blank: A revolutionary art theory, according to Thomasson, is… A. A theory suitable for ordinary art practice. B. A theory that explains art in a new way. C. A theory accepted by many artists. D. An easy-to-understand theory.",
      "definition": "B. A theory that explains art in a new way."
    },
    {
      "term": "Why doesn't the ontological model of natural kinds apply to works of art? A. Because works of art lack essential nature. B. Because works of art are always changing. C. Because works of art are created by humans. D. Because works of art cannot be distinguished by examining their composition.",
      "definition": "C. Because works of art are created by humans."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which book is authored by Clive Bell? A. The Role of Theory in Aesthetics B. The Principles of Art C. Art D. Institutional Theory of Art",
      "definition": "C. Art"
    },
    {
      "term": "What are the unresolved issues in Clive Bell's Formalist Theory? A. Failure to explain the significance of form and aesthetic emotion B. Art is a matter of form, not of content. C. He dismisses \"descriptive painting\" as not being art. D. All of the above viewpoints.",
      "definition": "D. All of the above viewpoints."
    },
    {
      "term": "Who was the first to distinguish \"artworks\" from \"handicrafts\"? A. I. Kant B. R.G.Collingwood. C. Clive Bell D. Monroe Beardsley",
      "definition": "B. R.G.Collingwood."
    },
    {
      "term": "What is an advantage in Collingwood's artistic view/perspective? A. It can be applied universally to all forms of art. B. Distinguishes artwork from art itself, essentially a spiritual rather than a material thing. C. Argues about \"entertainment art\". D. Argues about magic art.",
      "definition": "A. It can be applied universally to all forms of art."
    },
    {
      "term": "Who stated: \"An artwork is something created with the purpose of providing others with the satisfaction of aesthetic pleasure\"? A. Monroe Beardsley B. Oswald Hanfling C. George Schlesinger D. Richard Lind",
      "definition": "A. Monroe Beardsley"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Aristotle, what is tragedy? A. A story about fortunate events happening to people with good character. B. A story about absurd events happening to people with poor character. C. A story about unfortunate events happening to people with good character. D. A story about unfortunate events happening to people with poor character.",
      "definition": "C. A story about unfortunate events happening to people with good character."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Aristotle, what makes tragedy appealing? A. The characters in tragedy are famous individuals. B. Tragedy tells true stories. C. Tragedy has a plot arranged in a chain of cause and effect. D. Tragedy uses poetic language.",
      "definition": "C. Tragedy has a plot arranged in a chain of cause and effect."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Novitz, what is necessary to understand a fictional work correctly? A. Believe in the story. B. Treat the story as if it were real. C. Interpret and treat the story as if it were real. D. Implicitly understand that one is reading a fictional work.",
      "definition": "C. Interpret and treat the story as if it were real."
    },
    {
      "term": "What causes us to describe a work of art with emotions? A. Artworks can be described using language. B. Artworks can evoke emotions in the viewers. C. Artworks can be compared to other artworks. D. Artworks can be analyzed according to their constituent elements.",
      "definition": "B. Artworks can evoke emotions in the viewers."
    },
    {
      "term": "Why is the movie \"The Exorcist\" considered so scary, even though it is a work of fiction? A. Based on a true story. B. Evokes primal human fears. C. Uses many special effects. D. Filmed in a frightening location.",
      "definition": "B. Evokes primal human fears."
    },
    {
      "term": "The notion of art with an aesthetic function by Monroe Beardsley, Oswald Hanfling, George Schlesinger, and Richard Lind faces what issues? A. Overlooks many other purposes of art. B. Neglects the expressive aspect of art. C. Equates the aesthetic function of art with non-art. D. All of the above issues.",
      "definition": "D. All of the above issues."
    },
    {
      "term": "Who argued that defining art is at best a matter of describing what counts as an accepted work at any one time; the hope of finding some ‘real’ definition of art – some set of necessary and sufficient conditions – is a fruitless one, as that definition is always subject to change with the next artistic revolution? A. Oswald Hanfling did not argue as such. Oswald Hanfling believes that \"An artwork is a kind of creation whose primary function is to provide aesthetic satisfaction to others.\" B. R.G. Collingwood C. Paul Ziff D. Clive Bell",
      "definition": "C. Paul Ziff"
    },
    {
      "term": "Among the philosophers below, who argued that art is an 'open' concept? A. Monroe Beardsley B. Morris Weitz C. R.G. Collingwood D. Arthur Danto",
      "definition": "B. Morris Weitz"
    },
    {
      "term": "Fill in the blank: Arthur Danto argues that what is needed to perceive something like the Fountain as art is … the artwork itself is simply not achievable. A. Atmosphere of artistic theory B. Knowledge of art history C. Artworld. D. All of the above",
      "definition": "D. All of the above"
    },
    {
      "term": "Which of the following definitions is not by George Dickie? A. \"An artist is someone who participates with understanding in the making of a work of art.\" B. \"A work of art is an artifact of a kind created to be presented to an artworld public.\" C. \"A public is a set of persons the members of which are prepared in some degree to understand an object which is presented to them.\" D. \"A work of art is any creative arrangement made by one or more media, with the primary function of conveying an important aesthetic object.\"",
      "definition": "D. \"A work of art is any creative arrangement made by one or more media, with the primary function of conveying an important aesthetic object.\""
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Shusterman, how can the suppression of bad art to allow good art to flourish be justified? A. By arguing that bad art can harm society. B. By arguing that good art serves the ultimate moral benefit. C. By arguing that good art will always prevail over bad art. D. By arguing that bad art is a waste of time and money.",
      "definition": "B. By arguing that good art serves the ultimate moral benefit."
    },
    {
      "term": "Fill the blank:The relationship between the moral value and aesthetic value of an artwork, according to Anderson and Dean, is... A. They are one. B. They are unrelated. C. Moral value is more important than aesthetic value. D. Aesthetic value is more important than moral value.",
      "definition": "B. They are unrelated."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Clive Bell, what is art? A. It is a means to achieve a beautiful spiritual state. B. It is something that evokes aesthetic emotions. C. It is a human activity that brings moral benefits. D. All of the above answers are correct.",
      "definition": "B. It is something that evokes aesthetic emotions."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Anne Eaton, why is it 'naive' or 'mischievous' to remove the special position of the artist or critic from the equation? A. Because it would make art meaningless. B. Because it would perpetuate the 'default' dominant position in history, historically assigned to men and women corresponding roles. C. Because it would make art apolitical. D. Because it would make art amoral.",
      "definition": "B. Because it would perpetuate the 'default' dominant position in history, historically assigned to men and women corresponding roles."
    },
    {
      "term": "What impact has the feminist movement had on artistic philosophy? A. Spawned various ideological movements such as contextualism. B. Brought art back into a broader societal context. C. Raised questions about the ethical and political roles of art. D. All of the above answers.",
      "definition": "D. All of the above answers."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which statement is correct about Marcel Duchamp's artwork Fountain? A. Fountain was exhibited at the 1917 exhibition of the Society of Independent Artists in New York. B. Fountain is considered the most famous artwork of the 20th century. C. Fountain is Marcel Duchamp's first Readymade artwork. D. Fountain was rejected by the Society of Independent Artists at the 1917 exhibition.",
      "definition": "B. Fountain is considered the most famous artwork of the 20th century."
    },
    {
      "term": "Which information is not correct about the artist Marcel Duchamp? A. Duchamp is the author of the famous painting Les Demoiselles d'Avignon (1907). B. Marcel Duchamp is a famous sculptor of the 20th century. C. Before becoming a significant artist of Dadaism, Duchamp was involved in Futurism. D. Duchamp is considered one of the important artists of 20th-century Modern art.",
      "definition": "A. Duchamp is the author of the famous painting Les Demoiselles d'Avignon (1907). D. Duchamp is considered one of the important artists of 20th-century Modern art."
    },
    {
      "term": "Since when has the term 'Ars' been mentioned? A. Ancient Greece B. The Middle Ages C. The Renaissance D. The Enlightenment",
      "definition": "B. The Middle Ages"
    },
    {
      "term": "What role did the term 'Fine Art' play in the 18th century? A. Providing a definition of art B. Asserting the specificity of art C. Contributing to distinguishing art from other areas of skill D. It was in the 18th century that the concept of “fine art” emerged, gathering the Arts with a capital ‘A’ and distinguishing them from other areas of skill.",
      "definition": "C. Contributing to distinguishing art from other areas of skill"
    },
    {
      "term": "Who is considered the first to aim at defining Art? A. Aristotle B. Plato C. I. Kant D. St. Augustine",
      "definition": "C. I. Kant"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Taylor, why is the portrayal of black characters as victims in films harmful? A. Because it makes black people invisible. B. Because it reinforces the stereotype that black people are victims. C. Because it makes white people feel guilty. D. Because it makes black people feel inferior.",
      "definition": "B. Because it reinforces the stereotype that black people are victims."
    },
    {
      "term": "How did Louis Daguerre influence the artistic views of the 19th century? A. Triggered a revolution in the history of painting B. Affirmed art's departure from imitation C. Introduced a definition of art D. Changed the perception of expression in art",
      "definition": "A. Triggered a revolution in the history of painting B. Affirmed art's departure from imitation D. Changed the perception of expression in art"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Bicknell, what is the respectful attitude that performers and audiences should have when playing or listening to blues music? A. Focus on musical technique. B. Focus on the performer's emotions. C. Focus on the song's significance to the audience D. Focus on the history of blues music.",
      "definition": "C. Focus on the song's significance to the audience"
    },
    {
      "term": "\"What do 'feminist art' and the aesthetics of Black people have in common? A. No commonality B. Both focus on the beauty of the human body. C. Both are created by Black women. D. Both challenge the assumptions about the nature of art and artistic beauty.",
      "definition": "D. Both challenge the assumptions about the nature of art and artistic beauty."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Virginia Woolf, what is necessary for a woman to be able to write? A. Natural talent. B. Formal education. C. Natural talent and formal education. D. Money, a room of one's own, and educational attainment.",
      "definition": "D. Money, a room of one's own, and educational attainment."
    },
    {
      "term": "Who argued that \"to evaluate a new work of art, one must rely not only on its own characteristics but also on its relationship to previous ones\"? A. Jerrold Levinson B. Aristotle C. Collingwood D. George Schlesinger",
      "definition": "A. Jerrold Levinson"
    },
    {
      "term": "Who argued that \"works of art are not created out of nothing, but depend on preexisting art by adhering to, advancing, or challenging existing artistic traditions\"? A. Jerrold Levinson B. R.G. Collingwood C. George Dickie D. Morris Weitz",
      "definition": "A. Jerrold Levinson"
    },
    {
      "term": "Which information is incorrect about the artwork Black Square (1915)? A. Black Square is a work by the artist Kasimir Malevich. B. Black Square is a representative work of Futurism. C. Black Square is a representative work of Suprematism. D. Black Square was exhibited in the \"0-10\" exhibition in 1915.",
      "definition": "B. Black Square is a representative work of Futurism."
    },
    {
      "term": "Why did the art critic Alexander Benois describe the Black Square as 'aloof', 'arrogant', and 'ostentatious'? A. Because the painting is just a black square. B. Because it is the most representative painting of Suprematism. C. Because of its placement in the exhibition hall. D. Because Black Square is significant for the innovation in Russian art.",
      "definition": "C. Because of its placement in the exhibition hall."
    },
    {
      "term": "How can we evaluate a painting? A. It's bland, boring. B. It's dull. C. Whether it's beautiful or not. D. The painting is drawn in black within a square.",
      "definition": "C. Whether it's beautiful or not."
    },
    {
      "term": "\"Mr Blobby\" was awarded what title by the Ugly Animal Preservation Society in 2013? A. Most beautiful in the world B. the best at being ugly. C. the most intelligent D. the fiercest",
      "definition": "B. the best at being ugly."
    },
    {
      "term": "\"Mr Blobby\" is typically found at what depth below the ocean surface? A. 10 meters B. 100 meters C. 1,000 meters D. 10,000 meters",
      "definition": "C. 1,000 meters"
    },
    {
      "term": "What does the Blobfish eat? A. Algae B. Small fish C. Organic matter particles D. Aquatic plants",
      "definition": "C. Organic matter particles"
    },
    {
      "term": "According to Torquato Tasso, where does the beauty of art originate from? A. The creative ability of humans. B. The techniques and tools of the artist. C. Direct imitation of the beauty of nature. D. Religious belief and spirituality.",
      "definition": "C. Direct imitation of the beauty of nature."
    },
    {
      "term": "According to I. Kant, what creates the sense of the sublime? A. Art B. Nature C. Both art and nature D. Nothing",
      "definition": "B. Nature"
    },
    {
      "term": "Hegel agrees with which of Kant's views on beauty (found in nature or in art)? A. Nature is more beautiful than art. B. The beauty of art is a product of human intellect. C. Both art and nature possess beauty. D. The beauty of nature is the beauty of a particular external appearance.",
      "definition": "B. The beauty of art is a product of human intellect."
